===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc}} {{term|ἀναφορά||a carrying back|lang=grc|tr=anaphora|sc=polytonic}} from {{term|ἀνά||up|tr=ana|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} + {{term|φέρω||I carry|tr=pherō|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl2=anaphors|pl3=anaphora}}

# {{rhetoric}} The [[repetition]] of a [[phrase]] at the beginning of phrases, [[sentence]]s, or [[verse]]s, used for [[emphasis]].
#: ''An example of '''anaphora''' is Shakespeare’s “Mad world! Mad kings! Mad composition!”''
# {{linguistics}} An [[expression]] that [[refer]]s to another expression, especially a [[precede|preceding]] one. An example is a [[pronoun]] that refers to its [[antecedent]].
# {{plural of|anaphor}}
# {{plural of|anaphora}}

====Usage notes====
In linguistics, the terms {{term|anaphor}} and {{term||anaphora}} are sometimes used interchangeably, although in some theories, a difference is made between them.  See [[w:Anaphora (linguistics)|the Wikipedia article]].

